/-!
# The Čech complex is a coreflection onto the power-torsion objects

Let `(𝒞, 𝒪)` be a ringed site, let `f_1, …, f_r` be global sections of `𝒪` and let
`ℐ = (f_1, …, f_r) ⊂ 𝒪` be the sheaf of ideals they generate. Write

`Č(f) = (𝒪_{f_∅} → ∏_{i₀} 𝒪_{f_{i₀}} → ⋯ → 𝒪_{f_1 ⋯ f_r})`

for the extended alternating Čech complex of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/FormalGeometry/DerivedCompletionRinged/ExtendedCech.lean` and
`Č(f) ⟶ 𝒪` for its augmentation. This file proves that the augmentation is **universal among
morphisms from an `ℐ`-power-torsion object**: for every `T` in `D(𝒪)` whose derived
localizations `𝒪_{f_i} ⊗^𝐋_𝒪 T` all vanish, composing with the augmentation is a bijection

`Hom_{D(𝒪)}(T, Č(f)) ⟶ Hom_{D(𝒪)}(T, 𝒪)`.

Since `Č(f)` is itself such an object — `𝒪_g ⊗^𝐋_𝒪 Č(f) = 0` for every `g` divisible by one of
the `f_i`, which is
`RingedSite.isZero_derivedTensor_awayComplex_extendedAlternatingCech_prodGlobalSection`
— the augmentation is a **coreflection** of `D(𝒪)` onto those objects, and therefore pins
`Č(f) ⟶ 𝒪` down up to a unique isomorphism over `𝒪` by a universal property alone.

This is the sheaf-level counterpart of the ring-level
`Stacks.LocalCohomology.bijective_comp_extendedCechAugmentationHom`, which is [Stacks, Tag
0A6R]'s input on a ring; nothing in the source states it for a ringed site.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.DerivedPowerTorsion`: the `ℐ`-power-torsion condition on an
  object of `D(𝒪)`, namely that `𝒪_{f_i} ⊗^𝐋_𝒪 T` vanishes for every `i`.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.bijective_comp_cechAugmentation_of_derivedPowerTorsion`: **the
  universal property.** Composing with the Čech augmentation is a bijection on morphisms out of
  an `ℐ`-power-torsion object.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedPowerTorsion_extendedAlternatingCech`: `Č(f)` is
  `ℐ`-power-torsion.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.exists_iso_extendedAlternatingCech_of_universal`: **the
  coreflection is unique.** Any `κ : K ⟶ 𝒪` in `D(𝒪)` with `K` power-torsion and with the same
  universal property is isomorphic to `Č(f) ⟶ 𝒪` over `𝒪`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedPowerTorsion_iff`: the condition is a condition on the
  **cohomology sheaves** of `T`: `𝒪_{f_i} ⊗^𝐋_𝒪 T = 0` for every `i` exactly when
  `𝒪_{f_i} ⊗_𝒪 H^q(T) = 0` for every `i` and every `q`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.bijective_comp_cechAugmentation`: the universal property with
  the torsion hypothesis in the equivalent form "the derived base change
  `Stacks.Cohomology.derivedCoefficientChange` of `T` to each `𝒪_{f_i}` vanishes", which is the
  form the proof consumes.
* `DerivedRestrictScalars.isZero_of_isZero_restrictScalars_mapDerivedCategory`:
  restriction of scalars reflects the zero object of the derived category.

## Why this statement, and what it is for

[Stacks, Tag 099F] asserts that the inclusion of the derived-complete objects into `D(𝒪)` has a
left adjoint for an arbitrary **finite type** sheaf of ideals. The case of an ideal sheaf
generated by finitely many global sections is proved, as
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_generatedIdealSheaf`, and the
general case is reduced to producing a single `κ : K ⟶ 𝒪` in `D(𝒪)` with three properties, by
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_of_augmented`. The source
produces that `K` by [Stacks, Tag 099E], whose content is that `K` restricts to `Č(f)` over
every object on which `ℐ` is generated by a finite family `f` — and the obstruction to running
the source's construction here is that [Stacks, Tag 099D] identifies its complex with the Čech
complex by an isomorphism of the *derived* category `D(A)`
(`Stacks.LocalCohomology.FunctorialCechData.exists_cech_iso`). That identification is natural in
the ring — `Stacks.LocalCohomology.FunctorialCechData.cechIso_natural` — but a compatible family
of morphisms of `D(𝒪(V))`, one for each object `V`, is still not a morphism of the derived
category of presheaves of modules, so *that* identification gives no map of presheaf complexes
to sheafify.

A map of presheaf complexes is now available, from a different realisation of the same
construction: on the category `Stacks.GenPairSite.GenPair r` of commutative rings carrying a
chosen family of `r` elements, `Stacks.GenPairSite.cechToK` is a map of complexes of presheaves
of modules from the extended alternating Čech complex of the chosen family to the filtered
colimit `K(A, I)`, it is a quasi-isomorphism at every object
(`Stacks.GenPairSite.quasiIso_evaluation_cechToK`) and it carries the Čech augmentation to the
augmentation of `K` (`Stacks.GenPairSite.cechToKTerm_comp_kAugZero`). The assignment
`V/U ↦ (𝒪(V), f₁|_V, …, f_r|_V)` is a functor to that category, so `K_{U, I}` and the
identification are presheaf-level objects on `𝒞/U` and there *is* something to sheafify. What
stands between that and [Stacks, Tag 099E] is the independence of the sheafification from the
chosen finitely generated `I ⊆ ℐ(U)` and the descent of the resulting complexes from the slices
`𝒞/U` to `𝒞`; effective descent for sheaves of modules on a site is not proved in this library,
and neither is cover-density of the full subcategory of objects over which `ℐ` is finitely
generated, which is what `Stacks.DenseSubsiteModules.moduleRestrictionEquivalence` would need.

The universal property proved here routes around that: it characterises `Č(f) ⟶ 𝒪` with no
reference to any chosen map, so a complex on `𝒞` built by any means at all — in particular the
degreewise sheafification of `V ↦ K(𝒪(V), I₀𝒪(V))` — is identified with `Č(f)` over `𝒪` as soon
as it is power-torsion and has the same universal property. That identification is
`exists_iso_extendedAlternatingCech_of_universal`.

The statement is proved for an arbitrary ringed site, so it applies verbatim on the localized
ringed site `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.over X U` of [Stacks, Tag 04IX], which is the form
[Stacks, Tag 099E] uses it in: there `f` is a finite family of global sections of `𝒪_U`
generating `ℐ|_U`, which is the regime
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.exists_coversTop_eq_generatedIdealSheaf_of_isFiniteType`
delivers from `SheafOfModules.IsFiniteType`.

## The torsion condition

`DerivedPowerTorsion` is the vanishing of the derived localization at each generator. This is
the sheaf-level form of the ring-level `Dualizing.derivedPowerTorsionProperty`, which that
development's own
`Dualizing.PowerTorsionBaseChange.derivedPowerTorsion_iff_locDerived_isZero` identifies with
the vanishing of the derived localization at each element of a generating family.

Two facts make the condition visibly intrinsic to the cohomology of `T` rather than to the
complex:

* `derivedPowerTorsion_iff` reads it off the cohomology **sheaves**: it says precisely that
  inverting any one of the `f_i` kills every `H^q(T)`. The proof is the flat base-change
  comparison `Stacks.DerivedTensor.RingedSite.homologyTensorFlatIso`, whose flatness input is
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isFlatMap_toAwaySheaf`.
* The vanishing of `𝒪_{f_i} ⊗_𝒪 H^q(T)` is in turn equivalent to every local section of
  `H^q(T)` being annihilated, locally on the site, by a power of `f_i`, and hence — the `f_i`
  being finitely many — by a power of `ℐ`. **That last step is not proved here**, and it is the
  only gap between this hypothesis and the sectionwise phrase "the cohomology sheaves of `T`
  are `ℐ`-power-torsion". As a Lean proposition what is missing is
  `IsZero (((Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.sheafTensor X.O).obj M).obj (awayModule X g)) ↔
  Stacks.IdealPowerTorsion.sheafTorsion … = ⊤`, for a sheaf of `𝒪`-modules `M` and a global
  section `g`; it is a statement about a sheafified tensor product of sheaves of modules, with
  no bearing on anything below. Nothing in this file assumes it.

## The proof

The skeleton is the ring-level one, and the three category-theoretic steps of that proof are
reused verbatim, being stated for an arbitrary abelian category with a derived category:

1. **The stupid filtration.** `Stacks.LocalCohomology.bijective_comp_toSingle` turns the
   universal property into the vanishing of `Hom(T, Č(f)^i[q])` for every `i ≠ 0` and every
   `q`, the Čech complex being concentrated in the degrees `0, …, r` by
   `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isZero_extendedAlternatingCech_X_of_neg` and
   `…_of_lt`, and the augmentation being invertible in degree zero by
   `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isIso_cechAugmentationZero`.
2. **The terms.** `Č(f)^i` is the product of the `𝒪_{f_S}` over the subsets `S` of cardinality
   `i`, a product over a finite index type, so `Stacks.LocalCohomology.subsingleton_hom_pi`
   reduces the vanishing to one factor at a time. For `i ≠ 0` every such `S` is non-empty.
3. **One factor.** `𝒪_{f_S}` with `j ∈ S` is a sheaf of `𝒪_{f_j}`-modules, the factorisation
   `𝒪 ⟶ 𝒪_{f_j} ⟶ 𝒪_{f_S}` being pinned by the universal property
   `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.existsUnique_lift_toAwaySheaf` — `f_j` is invertible in
   `𝒪_{f_S}` by `isUnit_toAwaySheaf_prodGlobalSection_of_mem` — so it suffices that `T` admit no
   nonzero morphism into the image of `D(𝒪_{f_j})` in `D(𝒪)`.

Step 3's input is the base-change adjunction rather than a torsion computation:
`subsingleton_hom_restrictScalars_of_isZero_derivedCoefficientChange` reads
`Hom_{D(𝒪)}(T, N|_𝒪)` as `Hom_{D(𝒪_{f_j})}(T ⊗^𝐋_𝒪 𝒪_{f_j}, N)` through `Lf^* ⊣ Rf_*` of
[Stacks, Tag 07A6] at the coefficient change `𝒪 ⟶ 𝒪_{f_j}`, which exists with no flatness
hypothesis — the identification of `Rf_*` with restriction of scalars on derived categories is
the one `Stacks.SheafOfModules.DerivedRestrictScalars.preservesProducts_mapDerivedCategory`
already makes. Propagation along the factorisation is
`subsingleton_hom_restrictScalars_of_factorization`, the mirror of
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isZero_derivedSheafHom_restrictScalars_of_factorization`.

What connects that hypothesis to the derived tensor product — and so to the vanishing of
`𝒪_g ⊗^𝐋_𝒪 Č(f)` — is `isZero_derivedCoefficientChange_of_isZero_derivedTensor`: the
projection formula `Stacks.Cohomology.CoefficientChange.projectionFormulaIso` at the sheaf of
modules `𝒪_{f_j}` reads `T ⊗^𝐋_𝒪 𝒪_{f_j}` as the restriction of
`(T ⊗^𝐋_𝒪 𝒪_{f_j}) ⊗^𝐋_{𝒪_{f_j}} 𝒪_{f_j}`, which the unit constraint
`Stacks.DerivedTensor.RingedSite.DerivedTensorProduct.rightUnitIso` of [Stacks, Tag 0FPT]
collapses to the restriction of the derived base change; and restriction of scalars reflects
the zero object, by `isZero_of_isZero_restrictScalars_mapDerivedCategory`.

## Implementation notes

The derived tensor product is packaged twice in this development, once indexed by the ringed
site and once by its structure sheaf; the two bifunctors are compared by
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedTensorObjIso`, and the braiding and unit constraints are
read through `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedTensorProductDatumOfSite`. `DerivedPowerTorsion`
is stated in the site-indexed packaging, which is the one the Čech base change of
`CechBaseChange.lean` uses, so that
`derivedPowerTorsion_extendedAlternatingCech` is that result with `prodGlobalSection f {i}`
rewritten to `f i`.

No `@[stacks]` attribute is attached. [Stacks, Tag 0A6R] is a statement about a ring `A` and a
finitely generated ideal `I ⊂ A` — the existence of the right adjoint `RΓ_Z` of the inclusion
`D_{I^∞-torsion}(A) ⟶ D(A)` together with the Čech formula for it — and it is registered on the
declarations that state those two halves, `Dualizing.localCohomologyRightAdjoint` and
`Stacks.LocalCohomology.CechFormula.localCohomologyCechIso`. What is proved here is the
counterpart of its *input* on a ringed site, which that tag does not assert; and
[Stacks, Tag 099F] is the adjunction for a finite-type sheaf of ideals, whose own statement this
file does not reach. So neither tag is claimed.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0A6R](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0A6R)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 099B](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/099B)
